.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/clsx Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "clsx [![CI](https://github.com/lukeed/clsx/workflows/CI/badge.svg)](https://github.com/lukeed/clsx/actions?query=workflow%3ACI) [![codecov](https://badgen.net/codecov/c/github/lukeed/clsx)](https://codecov.io/gh/lukeed/clsx) [![licenses](https://licenses.dev/b/npm/clsx)](https://licenses.dev/npm/clsx)"
.PP
.RS 4
A tiny (239B) utility for constructing \fRclassName\fP strings conditionally\&.
.br
Also serves as a [faster](bench) & smaller drop-in replacement for the \fRclassnames\fP module\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
This module is available in three formats:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBES Module\fP: \fRdist/clsx\&.mjs\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBCommonJS\fP: \fRdist/clsx\&.js\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBUMD\fP: \fRdist/clsx\&.min\&.js\fP
.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save clsx
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
import clsx from 'clsx';
// or
import { clsx } from 'clsx';

// Strings (variadic)
clsx('foo', true && 'bar', 'baz');
//=> 'foo bar baz'

// Objects
clsx({ foo:true, bar:false, baz:isTrue() });
//=> 'foo baz'

// Objects (variadic)
clsx({ foo:true }, { bar:false }, null, { '\-\-foobar':'hello' });
//=> 'foo \-\-foobar'

// Arrays
clsx(['foo', 0, false, 'bar']);
//=> 'foo bar'

// Arrays (variadic)
clsx(['foo'], ['', 0, false, 'bar'], [['baz', [['hello'], 'there']]]);
//=> 'foo bar baz hello there'

// Kitchen sink (with nesting)
clsx('foo', [1 && 'bar', { baz:false, bat:null }, ['hello', ['world']]], 'cya');
//=> 'foo bar hello world cya'
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "clsx(\&.\&.\&.input)"
Returns: \fRString\fP
.SS "input"
Type: \fRMixed\fP

.PP
The \fRclsx\fP function can take \fI\fBany\fP\fP number of arguments, each of which can be an Object, Array, Boolean, or String\&.

.PP
.RS 4
\fBImportant:\fP \fIAny\fP falsey values are discarded!
.br
Standalone Boolean values are discarded as well\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
clsx(true, false, '', null, undefined, 0, NaN);
//=> ''
.fi
.PP
.SH "Modes"
.PP
There are multiple "versions" of \fRclsx\fP available, which allows you to bring only the functionality you need!
.SS "\fRclsx\fP"
.RS 4
\fBSize (gzip):\fP 239 bytes
.br
 \fBAvailability:\fP CommonJS, ES Module, UMD 
.RE
.PP

.PP
The default \fRclsx\fP module; see \fBAPI\fP for info\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { clsx } from 'clsx';
// or
import clsx from 'clsx';
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRclsx/lite\fP"
.RS 4
\fBSize (gzip):\fP 140 bytes
.br
 \fBAvailability:\fP CommonJS, ES Module
.br
 \fBCAUTION:\fP Accepts \fBONLY\fP string arguments! 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Ideal for applications that \fI\fBonly\fP\fP use the string-builder pattern\&.

.PP
Any non-string arguments are ignored!

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { clsx } from 'clsx/lite';
// or
import clsx from 'clsx/lite';

// string
clsx('hello', true && 'foo', false && 'bar');
// => "hello foo"

// NOTE: Any non\-string input(s) ignored
clsx({ foo: true });
//=> ""
.fi
.PP
.SH "Benchmarks"
.PP
For snapshots of cross-browser results, check out the [\fRbench\fP](bench) directory~!
.SH "Support"
.PP
All versions of Node\&.js are supported\&.

.PP
All browsers that support \fR\fRArray\&.isArray\fP\fP are supported (IE9+)\&.

.PP
>\fBNote:\fP For IE8 support and older, please install \fRclsx@1\&.0\&.x\fP and beware of \fR#17\fP\&.
.SH "Tailwind Support"
.PP
Here some additional (optional) steps to enable classes autocompletion using \fRclsx\fP with Tailwind CSS\&.

.PP
\fB\fP
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
Visual Studio Code 

.PP

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fRInstall the "Tailwind CSS IntelliSense" Visual Studio Code extension\fP
.IP "2." 4
Add the following to your \fR\fRsettings\&.json\fP\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
 "tailwindCSS\&.experimental\&.classRegex": [
   ["clsx\\\\(([^)]*)\\\\)", "(?:'|\\"|`)([^']*)(?:'|\\"|`)"]
 ]
}
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.RE
.PP

.PP
You may find the \fB`clsx/lite`\fP module useful within Tailwind contexts\&. This is especially true if/when your application \fBonly\fP composes classes in this pattern:

.PP
.PP
.nf
clsx('text\-base', props\&.active && 'text\-primary', props\&.className);
.fi
.PP
